E How to compile and run your program

Running the "make" command will produce the gerp executable which can then
be run using this command format: "./gerp inputDirectory outputFile". 
inputDirectory is the directory you'd like to search through and outputFile
is the file where the answers to your queries will be stored. The following
are all supported query commands which you can use after running the step
above:

"word" - typing any string followed by the return key uses case sensitive 
search to look through the provided directory

@i "word" or @insensitive "word" - preceding a word by @i or @insensitive 
looks through the provided directory for that given word using case
insensitive search

@q or @quit or Ctrl + d - ends the program

@f newOutputFilename - closes the current outputfile and opens the new 
file for future query outputs to be written to


F An “architectural overview,” i.e., a description of how your various program 
modules relate.For example, the FSTree implementation keeps a pointer to the 
root DirNode.

Our program is made up of three main parts that work together when the program 
is ran and during querying. We have a class called directoryProcessor that helps
traverse the file-system, a class called wrapper that indexes the file system, 
and a hashMap class. 
When the program starts, main passes the root directory to the Wrapper class. 
The Wrapper class uses the FSTree to walk the directory and intialize all the 
files into a vector that holds the file paths. The Wrapper class also reads the
contents of each file line by line and stores it into a 2d vector. That way 
each file and each line can be later retrieved by just an integer index. 

While the Wrapper class is reading the files, it strips non-alpanumeric 
characters from the edges of each word and inserts each word into two hash 
maps that are pivate members of the Wrapper class. One hashMap is 
case-sensitive, and one is case-insensitive.
The hashMaps are made of a struct that is owned by the hashMap class called 
WordInstance. This struct holds the name of the word, an integer to the file 
from the vector of filepaths, and also the integer to the exact line in the 
2d vector of lines. 

Once the indexing is done, main runs the query loop. It reads queries from 
the user and calls the search function that is owned by the Wrapper class. 
The search function returns the results of the search. 



G An outline of the data structures and algorithms that you used. Given 
that this is a data structures class, you need to always discuss any 
data structures that you used and justify why you used them. For this 
assignment it is imperative that you explain your data structures
and algorithms in detail, as it will help us understand your code since 
there is no single right way of completing this assignment.

We used vectors of strings to store the filepaths and also a 2d vector to 
store the lines of the files. Vectors was also used for our hashmap where we
made an array of type vector<wordInstance>. We chose to use vectors so that 
there is o(1) access to the different buckets so we can quickly retrieve the 
occurances of each word that the user is searching for in their query. With 
vectors we can easily just push_back() every new occurance of the words when 
creating the index. 

Another data structure we used was the hashmap. This was essential to actually 
be able to quickly lookup the word that the user is searching for. With the 
hashmap, each bucket is mapped to a key. The key is every word that is in the 
files. So when the user needs to lookup a word, we just hash the word and find 
the bucket and then print all the occurances of the in that bucket. Hashmaps 
allow us to have instant acccess to the bucket we need. We do not have to spend 
time searching through data to find the occurances of a word. One downside to 
hashmaps is collisions, where two different words are mapped to the same bucket 
by the hash function. In order to handle this, we used linear probing where if 
the bucket that the word was hashed to already holds a different word, then we 
look at the following buckets until we find an empty one. We had one hashmap 
that was case sensitive, which had keys that were every version of that word. 
The case insensitive hashmap just had the lowercase version of the words. 

Algorithms are essential to this program. The first thing the program does is 
build the index, and the algorithm for that is basically a recursive walk. I 
start at the root directory, and for every entry inside it I either recurse 
deeper if it's another directory, or open it and read it if it's a file. As 
I'm walking, I keep track of the path I've taken so far so that when I hit a 
file, I already know its full path and can store it. For each file I open, 
I read it line by line. I stash the whole raw line in my line storage so I 
can print it back later. Another essential algorithm is our resize function. 
Resizing is what keeps lookups fast as
the map fills up. I make a new bucket array that's double the size, reset my 
entry count to zero, and then I walk through
every entry in every bucket of the old array and re-insert it into the new one. 
The bucket each entry lands in changes because the modulus changed, so I have to
recompute all of them. Once everything's been moved over, the old array gets
thrown away and the map is back to having short chains.



H Details and an explanation of how you tested the various parts of your
classes and the program as a whole. You may reference the testing files 
that you submitted to aid in your explanation.

We started by building up the hashMap and processor files because that
they processor and store all the data that's used in queries later on.
We tested these classes using the provided unit testing framework. You
can find the specific tests in the unit_tests.h file. After words we 
built the wrapper and the query loop, which we tested using tinyData 
and the Gutenberg files and by passing various commands in. We tested
varioud edge cases like searching for an empty string using fully a
non-alpanumeric words, common words like the, different capitalizations
and mixtures of alphanumeric and non-alphanumeric characters, case 
insenitive search. After passing a specific edge-case commands through 
our implementation of gerp, we would diff the output file with the 
output file of the reference implementation ran with the same commands.


